const faqs = [
    {
        q: "What should I do immediately if my car breaks down on Sheikh Zayed Road?",
        a: "Move your vehicle to the hard shoulder or emergency lane as quickly and safely as possible. Switch on hazard warning lights immediately. If safe to do so, place a reflective warning triangle 50 metres behind the vehicle. Do not stand directly behind or in front of the car on active highway lanes; exit through the passenger side away from traffic and wait behind the safety crash barrier. Once in a secure position, call +971 56 612 1423 and provide your nearest road marker or green exit sign number so our team can dispatch assistance."
    },
    {
        q: "What is the difference between roadside assistance, vehicle recovery, and towing?",
        a: "Roadside assistance involves on-site mechanical and electrical troubleshooting (jump starts, puncture repairs, spare wheel mounting, or emergency fuel) to get your car driving again without towing. Vehicle recovery involves winching or safely extracting an immobilised vehicle from sand, ditches, or tight parking basements. Car towing or flatbed recovery involves loading a mechanically disabled or unsafe vehicle onto a truck to transport it to our Al Satwa workshop."
    },
    {
        q: "Is roadside assistance covered by car insurance or vehicle warranties in Dubai?",
        a: "Many comprehensive motor insurance policies and manufacturer warranties include roadside assistance coverage, but terms and response times vary significantly. Some policies only cover towing up to a certain kilometre limit, while others exclude battery replacement costs or have high deductibles. If your insurer has extended waiting periods or you need urgent mechanical assessment, Dubai Car Repair Service provides direct pay-as-you-go dispatch."
    },
    {
        q: "Do you provide 24/7 roadside assistance in Dubai?",
        a: "Dubai Car Repair Service provides roadside assistance during our confirmed workshop operating hours from 7:00 AM to 10:00 PM daily, including weekends and public holidays. For incidents occurring late at night outside these hours, we advise contacting RTA emergency assistance or your insurance provider's dedicated hotline. [CONFIRM: 24/7 emergency dispatch availability vs scheduled 7AM-10PM operating hours]"
    },
    {
        q: "Can you fix my car at the roadside or will I need towing?",
        a: "Most common roadside failures-including dead batteries, central tread punctures, fuel exhaustion, minor coolant loss, and basic electrical faults-are resolved directly at your location. If on-site testing reveals severe internal mechanical damage, seized brakes, transmission failure, or blown radiators, towing to our 222 Al Satwa Road workshop is necessary. We assess the fault first and explain all options transparently."
    },
    {
        q: "My engine overheated and a warning light is on -can I keep driving?",
        a: "No. Never continue driving an overheated car. In Dubai's summer climate, driving with a pegged temperature gauge can warp aluminium cylinder heads, blow head gaskets, or seize engine pistons within minutes. Pull over immediately into a safe area, shut off the engine, pop the bonnet latch without opening a hot radiator cap, and call us for roadside assessment."
    }
